<.header>
  Your profile
  <:subtitle>
    These details fill demand letters when a case does not override them. Import from your macOS Contacts Me card or enter them manually.
  </:subtitle>
</.header>

<div class="content-card mt-2 max-w-2xl">
  <div class="content-card-body">
    <.simple_form for={@form} id="claimant-profile-form" phx-change="validate" phx-submit="save">
      <.input field={@form[:name]} type="text" label="Your name" />
      <.input field={@form[:address]} type="textarea" label="Mailing address" rows="4" />
      <.input field={@form[:phone]} type="text" label="Phone number" />
      <.input field={@form[:email]} type="email" label="Email address" />
      <.input field={@form[:dnc_registration_date]} type="date" label="DNC registration date" />
      <.input field={@form[:small_claims_county]} type="text" label="Small claims county" />

      <:actions>
        <.button phx-disable-with="Saving...">Save profile</.button>
        <.button type="button" phx-click="import_from_contacts" class="btn-secondary">
          Import from Contacts
        </.button>
      </:actions>
    </.simple_form>

    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-600">
      You can also set environment variables such as
      <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1">DNC_MY_ADDRESS</code>
      and <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1">DNC_MY_PHONE</code>
      for CLI exports. Saved profile values take precedence over env vars in the web app.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="content-card mt-6 max-w-2xl">
  <div class="content-card-body">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-zinc-900">Chrome helper (USPS + eFileCA)</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
      USPS tracking pages often never hydrate in headless Chrome, so Refresh status opens
      the same URL in your real browser. This unpacked extension reads the finished page
      and posts it back to this local app. Only a tracking number you just clicked Refresh
      for is accepted. The same extension also opens
      <a href="https://california.tylertech.cloud/OfsEfsp/ui/landing" class="text-brand underline">
        eFileCA
      </a>
      from a case — sign in there with your Tyler Technologies account — and fills the
      new-case wizard up to review. It never clicks Submit.
    </p>
    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm text-slate-700">
      <li>Open <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1">chrome://extensions</code></li>
      <li>Turn on Developer mode</li>
      <li>Click Load unpacked and choose this folder:</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 break-all rounded-lg bg-slate-100 px-3 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-slate-800">
      {@chrome_extension_dir}
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-600">
      After installing or updating files, click Reload on the extension card, then reload
      this app. You should see “Browser helper connected” on a case. Click Refresh status
      for USPS, or Fill eFileCA form on a case; keep the helper tab open until the work
      finishes. After a reload, Chrome may ask you to allow access to tylertech.cloud —
      grant it.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="content-card mt-6 max-w-2xl">
  <div class="content-card-body">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-zinc-900">Safari helper (USPS + eFileCA)</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
      Safari installs this helper when you open its Mac app. Settings → Extensions has no
      separate installer, and the extension stays off that list until the app has been opened.
      It is named <strong>DNC Watchdog Helper</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 break-all rounded-lg bg-slate-100 px-3 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-slate-800">
      {@safari_helper_app}
    </p>
    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm text-slate-700">
      <li>
        Click <strong>Install in Safari</strong>. macOS may ask you to open the app the first time.
      </li>
      <li>
        In the helper window, click <strong>Quit and Open Safari Extensions Preferences…</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>Turn on DNC Watchdog Helper.</li>
      <li>
        Allow <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1">tools.usps.com</code>, <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1">usps.com</code>, <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1">california.tylertech.cloud</code>, <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1">127.0.0.1</code>, and <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1">localhost</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Reload this app in that same Safari window. A case should say “Browser helper connected”.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-600">
      If the extension is still missing from that list, turn on Safari → Settings → Advanced → <strong>Show features for web developers</strong>, then choose <strong>Develop → Allow Unsigned Extensions</strong>, and click Install in Safari again.
      Safari forgets Allow Unsigned Extensions every time it quits.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
      <.button type="button" phx-click="open_safari_helper">Install in Safari</.button>
      <.button type="button" phx-click="open_safari_xcode" class="btn-secondary">
        Open in Xcode
      </.button>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-600">
      After helper scripts change, run
      <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1">mix dnc.build_safari_helper</code>
      (or Run
      <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1">{Path.basename(@safari_xcode_project)}</code>
      from Xcode), quit Safari with Cmd+Q, and install again.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
